window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"19-08","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"19-08-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-01/","title_he":"א – ירושת הגר את הוריו הנוכרים","title_en":"The Convert's Inheritance from His Non-Jewish Parents","body_en":"<p>The Sages said: \"A convert who has converted is like (<i>damei</i>) a newborn child\" (Yevamot 22a), that is, from a legal standpoint, at the time of his conversion the convert's connection to his family members lapses. And so the law is regarding inheritance. Before the conversion, the convert would inherit his father, for the descendants of Noaḥ (<i>benei Noaḥ</i>) inherit their parents by Torah law. But when the convert chose to convert, he is like one who has been born anew, and therefore, without an explicit will of his non-Jewish parents granting him of their estate, by Torah law the convert does not inherit them. But the Sages were concerned that there might be converts who, in order to gain the inheritance, would revert to their old ways and conduct themselves as non-Jews; therefore they enacted that even without an explicit will converts inherit their non-Jewish parents (Kiddushin 17b; SA ḤM 283:1).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The Sages said in the Mishna (Demai 6:10) that if the convert inherited from his father objects of idolatry and objects that are not of idolatry, since by Torah law he does not inherit him, the objects of idolatry are not yet considered part of his inheritance, and therefore he can say to his non-Jewish brother: \"You take the objects of idolatry, and I the other objects.\" But if the objects of idolatry have already come into his possession, since the Sages enacted that the convert inherits his father, he is obligated to destroy them, and he cannot receive another portion of the inheritance in exchange for them, since it is forbidden for him to derive benefit from them (SA YD 146:4)."}]},{"slug":"19-08-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-02/","title_he":"ב – נכסי הגר לאחר פטירתו","title_en":"The Convert's Property After His Death","body_en":"<p>Since a convert who has converted is considered like a newborn child, his legal ties to his family have been severed, and therefore, if he died without leaving a will, his relatives from before the conversion do not inherit him. And since he has no heirs, upon his death all his property and all the debts owed to him become ownerless (<i>hefker</i>), and whoever is first to acquire them acquires them. And even if he converted with his children or with his brothers, they do not inherit him, since at the time of the conversion they are like ones born anew, and their family ties that are binding with regard to inheritance are void. Even a child who was born after the conversion, if his conception was before the conversion, does not inherit him, for regarding the inheritance tie with the father, the determining time is the beginning of the pregnancy; therefore, upon the death of his convert father, all his property becomes ownerless, and whoever is first to acquire it acquires it (Bava Batra 149a; SA ḤM 275:1).</p><p>From this halakha too we learn about the special status of the convert, in whom the power of choice is most prominent, and the natural arrangements of inheritance do not apply to him. And if he wants his children who converted with him to inherit him, he must choose this and write a will that will arrange it as a gift that he decided to give them one hour before his death.</p><p>However, in practice, today, as a result of the law, the convert's children or his biological relatives acquire his property even if he did not write a will, for since upon his death his property becomes ownerless, and according to the law only his children or relatives are entitled to use his property, they are the ones who acquire his property from the ownerless state. And if he has no children or relatives, the law establishes that the state acquires his property.</p><p>If the convert fathered another child after his conversion, since according to the Torah that child is his only heir, it does not help that according to the law his children who were born before his conversion are in possession of his property; and if he wants all his children to inherit him, he must write this in a will. Likewise, when the deceased is a Jew who fathered children from a non-Jewish woman who afterward converted together with them, according to halakha only his relatives who are Jewish from birth, such as his brothers, inherit him. And if he wants his convert children to acquire his property according to halakha, he will need to write a will that will arrange the distribution of his property.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In the next halakha it will be explained that the Sages are pleased with one who returns a debt owed to the convert to his sons. And the same law applies to a deposit (Tosafot, Ran, Tur, Shakh 127:2, and others, and unlike Rabbeinu Tam). And in Mishna Rishona (Shevi'it 10:9) he added that this is the law also regarding one who acquired the convert's property and returned it to the convert's son – the Sages are pleased with him.<br>There was an incident with the daughters of the great Amora Shmuel, who were taken captive by robbers in order to sell them as maidservants, and the captors led them from Babylonia to the Land of Israel. Two of them were saved from rape, and managed through their wisdom to come and declare before the Sages that they had not had relations, in such a way that their declaration was accepted, and they redeemed them and permitted them to marry even kohanim (Ketubot 23a). But his daughter Raḥel was raped by one of the captors, whose name was Issur. From this rape Raḥel became pregnant, and meanwhile Issur was aroused to repent, and he converted and they married. The son who was born to them grew up to be one of the Amora'im, and was called after his mother: Rav Mari bar Raḥel, because his conception was not in holiness, since at that time his father was still a non-Jew (Rashi, Berakhot 16a, s.v. \"bera de-vat Shmuel\"; Rashbam, Bava Batra 149a, s.v. \"de-Issur\"; and unlike Tosafot, Bava Batra 149a, s.v. \"Rav Mari\"). Years passed and the time came for Issur the convert to depart from the world, and at that time he had twelve thousand zuz that he had deposited with Rava; but since the conception of his son Rav Mari was not in holiness, he was not considered his son to inherit him, and if he died, his money would become ownerless and Rava would acquire it. Nor could he, through a \"will of a person on his deathbed\" (<i>tzava'at shekhiv me-ra</i>), instruct that his money be given to his son, because a \"will of a person on his deathbed\" is effective only for one who has a law of inheritance (Rosh, Rashba and Rema ḤM 256:1. And according to the Rif, Rambam and SA there, only with regard to his son did they decree that his will has no validity, so that people would not mistakenly think that he is an heir). Nor could he perform an ordinary <i>kinyan ḥalifin</i> (acquisition by exchange), because money is not acquired through ḥalifin. But in the end he succeeded in preventing his money from falling into Rava's hands by transferring his money to his son through a mode of acquisition called <i>\"odita\"</i> (acknowledgment), that is, by giving an acknowledgment that his money belongs to his son (Bava Batra 149a; SA ḤM 256:3).<br>The Rishonim disagreed as to why Rava did not need to fulfill Issur's wish by virtue of \"it is a mitzva to fulfill the words of the deceased.\" According to Rabbeinu Tam, the Ramban and the Rosh, the mitzva to fulfill the words of the deceased is an extension of the law of inheritance, and therefore it does not apply to a convert. And according to the Rivam and the Mordekhai, there is no distinction between a convert and the rest of Israel, but the mitzva to fulfill the words of the deceased applies only when the deceased deposited the money in advance with a third party in order that he transfer it to a particular person (Rema 256:1). And although in the next halakha we will learn that the Sages are pleased with one who returns the convert's loan to his son, here, where the son is a Jew from birth, since his mother is Jewish, there was from the outset no connection between the Jewish son and his father who converted, and it was not about him that it was said that the Sages are pleased with one who returns the loan of the convert father to his son (Shakh 127:8)."}]},{"slug":"19-08-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-03/","title_he":"ג – החזרת הלוואה של גר לילדיו","title_en":"Returning a Convert's Loan to His Children","body_en":"<p>The Sages said in the Mishna (Shevi'it 10:9): \"One who borrows from a convert whose sons converted with him – he need not return it to his sons, but if he returned it, the Sages are pleased with him.\" That is, a Jew who received a loan from a convert whose sons also converted with him, and afterward the convert died, is not obligated to return the debt of the loan to his convert sons, since when they converted they became like ones born anew, and they do not inherit their father. But if he returns the loan to his sons, the Sages are pleased with him, because he is conducting himself according to integrity and morality. However, there are situations in which, if he returns it, the Sages are not pleased with him, and the Rishonim disagreed as to which circumstances are being discussed (Kiddushin 17b). But since in practice, if he does not return the loan to the convert's children a <i>ḥillul Hashem</i> (desecration of God's name) will be caused, it is a mitzva to return the loan to them, in order to sanctify the name of Heaven and prevent a desecration of God's name. And the same law applies to returning a deposit and anything similar.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In Kiddushin 17b they raised a difficulty: in Mishna Shevi'it 10:9 they said that the Sages are pleased with one who returns a debt to the convert's sons, whereas in a baraita they said that the Sages are not pleased with him. And they answered: \"There is no difficulty: here, where his conception and his birth were not in holiness; there, where his conception was not in holiness but his birth was in holiness.\"<br>According to Ri (Tosafot there 18a, s.v. \"kan\") and the Ritva, if his birth was in holiness, the Sages are not pleased with him if he returns it, lest people mistakenly consider him his son with regard to levirate marriage (<i>yibum</i>). But when his birth was not in holiness, the Sages are pleased with him if he returns it. On the other hand, according to Rashi, the Rambam in his Commentary on the Mishna, and others, if his birth was in holiness, the Sages are pleased with him if he returns it, and if his birth was not in holiness, the Sages are not pleased with him. And so wrote SA 127:2.<br>According to Rashi, the Rash and the Rashba, when the Sages said in the baraita about one who returns a loan to the convert's sons that \"the Sages are not pleased with him,\" the meaning is that the Sages do not credit him for this, but they also do not object to what he did. And perhaps they are even satisfied with it, as Rav Yosef Messas wrote in Responsa Mayim Ḥayim ḤM 27. On the other hand, according to the Ramban and the Ran, the meaning of \"the Sages are not pleased with him\" is that the Sages are not satisfied with what he did, like one who returns a lost object to a non-Jew, who thereby equates him to the law of returning a lost object to a Jew. Thus far. However, when the purpose is to sanctify the name of Heaven, and all the more so when there is a concern of desecration of God's name, it is a mitzva to return the debt, as they said regarding returning a lost object to a non-Jew (SA ḤM 266:1). Today it is nearly certain that if they do not return the loan to the convert's children, there will be resentment against the halakha and a desecration of God's name, and therefore in all situations it is a mitzva to return the loan (similar to this wrote the Ḥazon Ish, ḤM siman 21, s.v. \"149a\")."}]},{"slug":"19-08-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-04/","title_he":"ד – חובת הגר בכיבוד הוריו הנוכרים","title_en":"The Convert's Obligation to Honor His Non-Jewish Parents","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva for the convert to honor his non-Jewish parents, and it is forbidden for him to disgrace them (Rambam, Hilkhot Mamrim 5:11; SA YD 241:9). And although the mitzva of honoring parents, with its stringent parameters, does not apply to him, in everything that natural morality requires, the convert too is obligated toward his parents. And if he goes further and honors them more than natural morality requires, each place according to what is accepted there, he will thereby have <i>hiddur mitzva</i> (an enhancement of the mitzva). But if his parents are liable to influence him or his children for the worse, he should honor them as natural morality requires and no more (see the next halakha).</p><p>Since accepted morality obligates all human beings, Ḥam was therefore punished for disgracing Noaḥ his father (Bereishit 9:25). And when a person goes further and honors his parents more than accepted morality requires, he has merit in this, as is told of Dama ben Netina, who was a Roman official and was very careful about honoring his father, not to wake him even for the sake of selling precious stones at a very high price. And God gave him his reward, in that a red heifer (<i>para aduma</i>) was born in his herd and he could have received an enormous sum for it, but since he did not want to benefit from the reward for honoring his father, he was satisfied with the sum he would have received for the precious stones (Kiddushin 31a).</p><p>And although the convert is considered like one born anew, this does not mean that he is exempt from his previous obligations; rather, he has risen to a higher and more sanctified level. Therefore all the obligations that natural morality imposes on him remain upon him. Not only that, but if after he converts he violates them, there will be a desecration of God's name in this, for people will say that the one converting went \"from a more stringent holiness to a lesser holiness\": while he was a non-Jew he was obligated to honor his parents, whereas when he converted he was exempted from his obligation, as if the holiness of the Torah of Israel obligates less than the holiness of the morality accepted among the nations. And so we find with Avraham Avinu, who by the command of the Creator left his father and went up to the Land of Israel, and the Sages said (Bereishit Rabba 39:7) that he was worried and said: \"If I go out and leave my father, people will desecrate the name of Heaven through me and say: 'He left his father and went away in his old age.'\" Therefore it is stated (Bereishit 12:1): \"And the Lord said to Avram: Go forth from your land and from your birthplace and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you.\" \"The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: <i>Lekh lekha</i> (go forth for yourself) – I exempt you from honoring father and mother, but I do not exempt another from honoring father and mother.\" That is, because of the importance of the mitzva of going up to the Land, God exempted him from his obligation toward his father. And some added that Avraham was exempt from honoring his father because Teraḥ his father was wicked, for he did not agree to join in faith in the one God, and under Nimrod's pressure he even agreed to hand his son over to the fiery furnace; and nevertheless Avraham Avinu was concerned about a desecration of God's name, and God instructed him to go up to the Land (Ḥida, Devash Le-fi 1:39; Panim Yafot, Bereishit 12:1).</p><p>Therefore, it is forbidden for the convert to disgrace his parents, and all the more so it is forbidden for him to strike them. And it is a mitzva for him to treat them with respect, and to help them in their illness or old age, as accepted morality requires.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"It is forbidden for the convert to disgrace his parents, as the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Mamrim 5:11): \"A convert is forbidden to curse his non-Jewish father and to strike him, and he should not disgrace him, so that people will not say: 'We have come from a more stringent holiness to a lesser holiness,' for this one disgraces his father; rather, he treats him with some honor.\" And so wrote SA YD 241:9. However, the Sefer Ha-ḥinukh 260 wrote that since the obligation is based on the reasoning of \"we have come from a holiness,\" its force is rabbinic. And so wrote the Minḥat Ḥinukh there, 260:9. And so it is explained in Nazir 61a that non-Jews are not commanded concerning honoring parents. And so they said in Kiddushin 31a regarding Dama ben Netina, who was very meticulous in honoring his father, and the Sages said that he \"is not commanded and does.\" And indeed, several poskim wrote that the descendants of Noaḥ are not obligated in honoring father and mother (Or Same'aḥ, Hilkhot Mamrim 5:11; Beit Hillel YD 241:9).<br>But whatever natural morality requires obligates the descendants of Noaḥ as well. As Rav Nissim Gaon wrote in his introduction to the Talmud: \"All the mitzvot that depend on reason and on the understanding of the heart, everyone has already been obligated in them from the day that God created man on the earth, he and his descendants after him for all generations.\" And so wrote the Kli Ḥemda (Yitro 7) and in his book Ḥemdat Yisrael (1, Ner Mitzva, p. 91), that although the descendants of Noaḥ are not obligated in the parameters of the mitzva of honoring father and mother as Israel is, non-Jews are forbidden to disgrace their parents by virtue of natural morality. And he added that all the prohibitions about which \"cursed\" is stated in the portion of Ki Tavo obligate the descendants of Noaḥ as well, and are included in the mitzva of <i>dinim</i> (establishing laws and courts). And so wrote the Netziv in his approbation to Ahavat Ḥesed, that all matters of morality that reason and nature require, the descendants of Noaḥ are obligated in them. Therefore, the descendants of Noaḥ are obligated to honor their parents in everything that natural morality requires. And so wrote other Aḥaronim, that the descendants of Noaḥ are obligated in honoring parents (Parashat Derakhim, Zuta, Derekh Ha-atarim 8; Yad Efrayim, Shonot, siman 4; Derashot Maharash, p. 102, and others). Similarly, several Aḥaronim wrote that although the descendants of Noaḥ are not obligated in the mitzva of honoring father and mother, they must honor their parents by virtue of their manners, that is, by virtue of accepted morality (Ḥida, Ru'aḥ Ḥayim, Derush 5 for Shabbat Ha-gadol; Pri Megadim, Iggeret Petiḥa 3:20; Haggahot Rabbi Akiva Eiger 241:9; Zaken Aharon 2:87; Aseh Lekha Rav 6:62). And some say that the mitzva of honoring parents is a branch of the prohibition of blaspheming God, which is one of the seven commandments of the descendants of Noaḥ, since the Torah equated the honor of parents with the honor of Heaven (Malbim, Devarim 27:16; Naḥal Eshkol, Hilkhot Gerim 39, ot 6).<br>In summary: the convert's obligation to honor his non-Jewish parents is by virtue of natural morality and not from the parameters of the mitzva in the Torah; therefore the parameters of the obligation are rabbinic, but its foundation is at the level of a Torah mitzva, and perhaps even more. As Rav Glasner wrote (Dor Revi'i, introduction to Tractate Ḥullin): \"For whatever is abhorrent in the eyes of humanity at large, even though there is no explicit prohibition of the matter in the Torah, is forbidden to us more than the prohibitions of the Torah, and not only because of desecration of God's name.\" Similarly, the Sages said (Berakhot 35b) that there is no need for a verse to teach the obligation to recite a blessing before eating, because reason requires it; but in practice, since this obligation is not based on a verse, its parameters are rabbinic.<br>Since the convert's obligation to honor his parents is by virtue of natural morality, it does not obligate with the stringency of the mitzva in Israel. For example, among Israel the mitzva of honoring parents requires feeding them whenever they need assistance in eating and drinking. And this mitzva obligates even if they behaved improperly toward him, and according to many even if they are completely wicked. Whereas among the descendants of Noaḥ the obligation is according to accepted morality. Similarly, among Israel it is a mitzva to say things in the name of the parents in order to honor them, and not to contradict their words or call them by their name, whereas among the descendants of Noaḥ the mitzva is only as accepted morality requires (see SA YD 240:4-5; 10; 18; Peninei Halakha, Likkutim, Mishpaḥa 1:8; 14; 16). A Jew is forbidden to give a parent medical treatment that may draw blood from him, and only in a pressing situation is one lenient about this, whereas for the descendants of Noaḥ there is no prohibition in this (SA and Rema YD 240:3; Peninei Halakha there 1:24).<br>Even when parents converted with their children, the obligation of honoring parents according to its legal parameters does not apply to them. But since they brought their children to conversion, there is a moral obligation on their children to honor them at least as all of Israel must honor their parents and their rabbis, because they gave birth to them and raised them, and also brought them under the wings of the Shekhina. However, since the obligation is not a scriptural decree, when necessary they may give their parents medical treatment that draws blood."}]},{"slug":"19-08-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-05/","title_he":"ה – עניינים נוספים בכיבוד הורים","title_en":"Further Matters in Honoring Parents","body_en":"<p>Sometimes the convert's parents weaken him in his new path as a Jew, and sometimes the convert is not worried for himself, but is worried that his parents will influence his children. In such situations, it is proper for the convert to set his residence far from them, and thus, without hurting his parents, he will distance himself and his children from their influence. And if he is worried that even during visits with them they will influence his children for the worse, he should reduce the visits to the point at which he will still not be considered ungrateful toward them. And if they are hostile to him because of his conversion, he may refrain from visiting them at all, and only if they need help in their illness should he assist them as accepted morality requires. And if they are ill and ask to see their grandchildren before their death, it is proper for him to travel with his children to visit them (see Igrot Moshe YD 2:130).</p><p>When the convert's parents live abroad, it is a mitzva for the convert to make aliya to the Land like all of Israel, and thereby the concern that his parents will influence his children for the worse will be removed. Nevertheless, in special cases, if the convert has the means for it, it is proper for him to travel to visit them, for example when one of them is ill or when a special celebration is being held for him [?].</p><p>When the convert's parents are ill, even though they believe in another religion, it is fitting for their son to pray for their recovery. And if they wish it, it is fitting that he even ask the gabbai to say a \"Mi She-berakh\" for their recovery.<sup>5</sup></p><p>If the convert's parents take an interest in Judaism, it is good for him to bring them closer to the mitzvot of the Torah. And if they are happy to participate with him in Shabbat meals, it is good to include them in the meals. In this way, the concern that the parents will influence the grandchildren for the worse diminishes, and on the contrary, it is even possible that they will encourage the grandchildren to cleave to Judaism and the mitzvot of the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The poskim wrote that it is permitted for a Jew to pray for his non-Jewish partner to be healed of his illness (Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi in Responsa Ḥayim Ba-yad 33; Rov Dagan by Rabbi Yehuda Attia). And the same law applies to a Christian, for the descendants of Noaḥ were not commanded regarding <i>shituf</i> (associating another being with God) (Beit She'arim YD 229; Dibrot Eliyahu 7:51, who cited further sources). And likewise it is permitted for a convert to pray for his Christian father (Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:60; Rav Price in Mishnat Avraham on Sefer Ḥasidim vol. 3, p. 216, and many others)."}]},{"slug":"19-08-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-06/","title_he":"ו – אבלות","title_en":"Mourning","body_en":"<p>The mitzva that obligates mourning for first-degree relatives applies only to Jews. Therefore, even when an entire family converted together, since everyone who converts is like one born anew, their previous family ties do not obligate them to mourn for one another (Rambam, Hilkhot Evel 2:3; SA and Rema YD 374:5). All the more so, the convert is not obligated to mourn for his non-Jewish relatives. Likewise, a Jew whose Jewish father fathered children from a non-Jewish woman is not obligated to mourn for his brothers who are not Jewish.</p><p>But all this is from the standpoint of obligation; if he wishes, the convert may mourn for his non-Jewish parents or brothers as is accepted among them, and if he wishes to sit shiva for them according to the custom of Israel, he may. And when his friends know that one of his relatives has died, and if he continues to work and conduct himself as usual it will be an affront to the honor of the deceased, it is fitting that he mourn for him as is accepted.</p><p>When his parents or brothers also converted, although he is not obligated to mourn for them, it is fitting that he honor them and sit shiva for them, and observe all the customs of mourning for them, from <i>keri'a</i> (tearing the garment) to the mourning of the thirty days and the year.</p><p>It is proper for the convert to say the Mourner's Kaddish for his parents in the year of their death. And even if they did not convert, it is fitting that he show gratitude to them for having given birth to him by saying Kaddish for the elevation of their souls. And all the more so, if they converted with him, it is a mitzva for him to say Kaddish for the elevation of their souls.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"According to Maharam of Rothenburg (Hilkhot Semaḥot 85) and the Mordekhai (Mo'ed Katan, siman 907) in the name of Ri (of Lunel, according to the Derisha), the convert must mourn for his mother who converted, for since mourning on the first day is from the Torah, there is concern of <i>\"ati le-aḥlufei\"</i> (that people will come to confuse the cases): if he does not mourn for her, Jews too will learn to be lenient not to mourn for their parents. And since there is no first-day mourning without shiva, he should mourn all seven days. The Baḥ, the Derisha 3 and the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 11 wrote that this is the law according to the Rambam as well, since he too holds that mourning on the first day is from the Torah. According to them, this obligation applies only to his relatives on his mother's side, because among non-Jews only maternal relatives are considered related to one another and not paternal relatives; therefore, according to all opinions, he need not mourn for his father and for his relatives on his father's side who converted with him (see Rambam, Hilkhot Evel 2:3; SA YD 374:5). On the other hand, according to the Rema (YD 374:5, Darkhei Moshe 2), the convert need not mourn even for his mother and her relatives, because the halakha follows the Rosh and those who hold like him, that even mourning on the first day is rabbinic; and so wrote the Shakh 4 and the Ḥokhmat Adam 161:2. In addition, some say that even according to those who hold that mourning on the first day is from the Torah, the convert need not mourn for his mother who converted (Radbaz on Rambam, Hilkhot Evel 2:3).<br>In practice, since in a doubt concerning the laws of mourning one is lenient, and the decree of \"ati le-aḥlufei\" is also rabbinic, the convert is not obligated to sit shiva for his relatives on his mother's side.<br>And although by the letter of the law he is not obligated in this, Rav Yissakhar Tamar wrote that if the convert wants to observe all the laws of mourning for his relatives who converted, it is fitting to encourage him in this; otherwise, \"there is no greater wronging of the convert than this\" (Alei Tamar, Yevamot 2:6, s.v. \"Rabbi Yehuda omer\"). And even if his relatives did not convert, if he wants to be stringent with himself and sit shiva for them, he may (Ḥazon Ovadia, Avelut vol. 2, p. 119; Ha-avelut Ve-ha-niḥumim 6:4, by Rav Barukh Yashar, and many others). And some wrote without qualification that if the convert's father did not convert, \"he should not sit shiva for him, even as a matter of respect\" (Ashrei Ha-ish, rulings of Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv, EH vol. 2, 7:2, and others). But it seems that even according to them, in order to prevent shame or a family slight, for example when it is known that they died and it is not known that he is a convert, he may mourn for them (Geirut Ke-hilkhata 8:18, note 22; Mishnat Ha-ger 12:62).<br>When prayers are held in the house of the convert who is mourning for his relatives who converted, in order not to offend their honor, they should conduct themselves as is accepted in a house of mourners, without any change. However, a convert who mourns for his relatives, even if they converted, does not have the law of an <i>onen</i> (a mourner before the burial); therefore, between the death and the burial he remains obligated in positive commandments, and he must pray and recite blessings as usual, and on the first day he must put on tefillin. And if he does not want people to know that he is a convert, and he is worried that if they see that he put on tefillin they will understand that he is a convert, he should put on tefillin privately.<br>Regarding Kaddish: some say that it is not fitting for the convert to say Kaddish for his parents who did not convert, because Kaddish is intended for the elevation of the soul of the deceased, and this is not relevant to the soul of a non-Jew (Mishneh Halakhot 19:155; Rav Eliyahu in Divrei Mordekhai, Bereishit, p. 358). On the other hand, according to many, it is fitting for the convert to say Kaddish for his parents, even if they did not convert. So wrote Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:60; Zaken Aharon 2:87 (by Rav Aharon Walkin); Naḥalat Tzvi vol. 1, p. 118, and many others. And according to Rav Price (Mishnat Avraham on Sefer Ḥasidim vol. 3, p. 216), the convert is obligated to say Kaddish for his non-Jewish parents, for this is included in the honor with which the convert must honor his parents."}]},{"slug":"19-08-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-07/","title_he":"ז – פרו ורבו","title_en":"Peru U-revu","body_en":"<p>A convert who fathered children before his conversion has fulfilled through them the mitzva of <i>peru u-revu</i> (be fruitful and multiply). And although the descendants of Noaḥ are not obligated in the mitzva of peru u-revu as Israel is, the descendants of Noaḥ have a mitzva to engage in the settlement of the world, so that the world will endure and develop and not be desolate, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 45:18): \"He did not create it to be a waste; He formed it to be inhabited.\" And God blessed Noaḥ and his descendants that they would fulfill this mitzva and be fruitful and multiply, as it is stated (Bereishit 9:1): \"And God blessed Noaḥ and his sons, and said to them: Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth.\" And since he fulfilled the mitzva to settle the world, he has in practice also fulfilled the mitzva of peru u-revu. And although from a halakhic-legal standpoint a convert who has converted is considered like a newborn child, in the mitzva of settling the world one follows nature, and from the standpoint of nature the children he fathered while still a non-Jew are his children, and therefore he has fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu.</p><p>Some say that even if his children did not convert, he has fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu (Maharil, and so it emerges from other Rishonim). And some say that only if they converted has he fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu (Rambam; SA EH 1:7).<sup>7</sup></p><p>But a Jew who fathered children from a non-Jewish woman has not fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu, because they are non-Jews and their lineage does not follow him. And some say that if they convert, they will thereby rectify his sin and he will fulfill through them the mitzva of peru u-revu.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"In Yevamot 62a it is cited that according to Rabbi Yoḥanan, one who fathered children when he was a non-Jew and then converted has fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu, since in practice he has children. Therefore the first child who will be born to him as a Jew will not be considered a firstborn for inheritance. And according to Reish Lakish, he has not fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu, since \"a convert who has converted is like a newborn child.\" And the halakha follows Rabbi Yoḥanan. The Turei Even (Rosh Ha-shana 28a) explained that although while he was a non-Jew he was not commanded in peru u-revu, he was obligated in the mitzva of \"He formed it to be inhabited\" (<i>la-shevet yetzarah</i>), that is, in engaging in the settlement of the world, and since he fulfilled the mitzva of <i>shevet</i> (settlement), it was effective for him for the mitzva of peru u-revu. Our master Rav Kook added (Da'at Kohen 148) that although a convert who has converted is like a newborn child, in the mitzva of shevet one follows the nature of the world, and from the standpoint of nature he is considered the same person and his children are his children. Similarly, the Minḥat Ḥinukh (1:14) wrote that the mitzva of peru u-revu does not depend on the act of intercourse, which is a preparation for the mitzva, but on his having children; and since he has children, it turns out that he has fulfilled the mitzva through them. And some say that although in order to fulfill peru u-revu one must have intercourse, if children were born from acts of intercourse that he performed at a time when he was not commanded in this, he is exempt from the obligation of the mitzva (Sdei Ḥemed, Ma'arekhet Yud, 34, in the name of Yad Ha-melekh; Igrot Moshe EH 2:18).<br>Some say that even if his children who were born to him while he was a non-Jew did not convert, he has fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu. So wrote Responsa Maharil (196), and so it is implied from the Rishonim who wrote the view of Rabbi Yoḥanan without adding the condition that the children converted, among them: Rif, Rid and Riaz. And so the Beit Shmuel (EH 1:12) inferred from Tosafot (Yevamot 62a, s.v. \"Rabbi Yoḥanan\"). And so it is implied from the Ḥelkat Meḥokek (se'if katan 9). And there is room to say that this is the view of those who hold that the descendants of Noaḥ are obligated in the mitzva of peru u-revu (She'iltot, Rashi, Rivan and Meiri; see Simḥat Ha-bayit U-virkhato 6:9, 11 [?]).<br>On the other hand, some say that only if his children who were born to him while he was a non-Jew converted has he fulfilled through them the mitzva of peru u-revu. This is the view of the Rambam (Hilkhot Ishut 15:6), the Maggid Mishneh (there); the Meiri (Yevamot 62a, s.v. \"hayu lo\"); Rabbi Avraham Min Ha-har (Yevamot there); the Rosh (Yevamot 6:7); the Tur and SA (EH 1:7); the Levush, Knesset Ha-gedola and Yam Shel Shlomo (Yevamot 6:28). And the Arukh Ha-shulḥan (EH 1:19-20) narrowed the dispute and wrote that all agree that even when his children did not convert he is exempt from the mitzva of peru u-revu, but the novelty of the Rambam is that if they converted, he has fulfilled the mitzva, and not merely been exempted from the obligation."},{"n":8,"en":"The children whom a Jew fathered from a non-Jewish woman are not considered his children (Mishna Yevamot 2:5). Therefore he does not fulfill through them the mitzva of peru u-revu; so wrote the Yad Aharon (Alfandari) EH, Haggahot Beit Yosef 11, in the name of the Maharashdam. And so it emerges from many poskim, who wrote that it is desirable to convert his non-Jewish partner so that the Jew will be able to fulfill with her the mitzva of peru u-revu. Among them: Rav Shlomo Eiger (Responsa YD 34), Rav Noaḥ Segal (Olat Noaḥ EH 2), Rav Yosef Messas (Otzar Ha-mikhtavim 1:446); see Masoret Ha-giyur (5, end of 22). And so wrote Rav David Mo'ati (Responsa Tzuf Devash YD 56; Masoret Ha-giyur 25:2). And so wrote the Yad Peshuta (Hilkhot Ishut 15:1).<br>Seemingly, even if the children convert, he will not fulfill through them the mitzva of peru u-revu. So wrote Rav Noaḥ Segal (Masoret Ha-giyur 23:10). But Rav Glasner held that if his children convert, it is possible that he fulfills through them the mitzva of peru u-revu (Masoret Ha-giyur 21:19, p. 497)."}]},{"slug":"19-08-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-08/","title_he":"ח – איסורי עריות לגרים","title_en":"Prohibitions of Forbidden Relations for Converts","body_en":"<p>The Sages said: \"A convert who has converted is like a newborn child\" (Yevamot 22a). That is, from a legal standpoint, at the time of his conversion the convert's connection to his family members lapses. Therefore the severe death penalties that apply to those who sin in forbidden relations (<i>gilui arayot</i>) with family relatives do not apply to him. But the prohibition itself remains in place, for all the relatives who were forbidden to him within the framework of the seven commandments of the descendants of Noaḥ remain forbidden to him even after the conversion. For example, a brother and sister who converted: since they were forbidden to marry while they were non-Jews, even after their conversion they are forbidden to marry. For it is impossible that as a result of their elevation to the holiness of Israel, something that was forbidden to them as non-Jews should now become permitted; for if so, there would be a desecration of God's name in this, for people would say: \"We have come from a more stringent holiness to a lesser holiness!\"</p><p>In addition, the Sages forbade the convert to marry a female relative who was not forbidden to him while he was a non-Jew, if there is concern that as a result people will mistakenly think that it is permitted for Israel as well. For example, a non-Jew is permitted to marry his brother's divorcée, whereas among Israel this is forbidden; and the Sages forbade the convert to marry the divorcée of his convert brother, so that people will not mistakenly think that it is permitted for Israel as well.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In the Gemara in Yevamot 22a it is explained that although \"a convert who has converted is like a newborn child,\" the Sages decreed that the convert may not marry his female relative who was forbidden to him while they were non-Jews, lest people say: \"We have come from a more stringent holiness to a lesser holiness.\" And so ruled the Rambam, Hilkhot Issurei Bi'ah 14:11-12; and SA YD 269:1. Further, in the Gemara there, 97b, the Amora'im disagreed whether a convert may marry the wife of his maternal brother, when his brother married her after the conversion. As halakha it was ruled that it is forbidden because of \"ati le-aḥlufei,\" that is, lest onlookers mistakenly think that it is permitted for Israel as well (Rambam, Hilkhot Issurei Bi'ah 14:13; SA 269:3). However, regarding <i>sheniyot la-arayot</i> (secondary forbidden relations) they did not decree, because for Israel they are forbidden only by rabbinic law (Yevamot 22a; SA 269:8). And there are additional family members about whom the Sages did not decree; see SA 269:3. But in practice today they are forbidden, since among the non-Jews too they are included in the prohibitions of forbidden relations, and it is impossible that they would become permitted after the conversion (see Sefer Ḥasidim 829; and see above note 4. See further Ritva, Yevamot 22a, that some say that whatever is forbidden to marry according to the religion of those non-Jews remains forbidden after their conversion, and there 97b, that some say it is permitted. And it is possible that the discussion there concerns prohibitions due to the laws of their religion, but if they were due to natural morality, according to all opinions they would remain forbidden).<br>The prohibition of <i>yiḥud</i>, according to which an unrelated man and woman are forbidden to be secluded alone together lest they come to sin, does not apply to family members who converted. For although from a legal standpoint they are like ones born anew, the natural relationship remains as it was, and just as there is no prohibition of yiḥud for first-degree Jewish family relatives, so too for converts there is no prohibition. Therefore, the question of the seclusion of the convert with his female relatives did not arise at all among the Rishonim or the Aḥaronim, and only in the year 5741 (1981) was Igrot Moshe (EH 4:64) asked for the first time whether perhaps they have a prohibition of yiḥud. And he responded that the convert is like a newborn for legal matters, but in terms of natural feelings they are like family, and therefore they have no prohibition of yiḥud. However, some of the Aḥaronim of our times introduced a new stringency, claiming that since the Sages said that \"a convert who has converted is like a newborn child,\" his nature too changes (in Mishneh Halakhot 17:31 he was completely stringent; and in Shevet Ha-Levi 9:260 and Le-horot Natan 7:87 they were stringent regarding the Torah prohibitions of yiḥud). And there is no need to be concerned for their words."}]},{"slug":"19-08-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-09/","title_he":"ט – גיורת לכהן","title_en":"A Convert Woman and a Kohen","body_en":"<p>There are special mitzvot for kohanim, in order to set them apart and sanctify them for the holy service. Among the prohibitions specific to kohanim is that a kohen may not marry a divorced woman or a woman who had relations with a man whom she is forbidden to marry (<i>zona</i>). As it is stated (Vayikra 21:6-7): \"They shall be holy to their God, and they shall not profane the name of their God, for the fire-offerings of the Lord, the bread of their God, they offer, and they shall be holy. A woman who is a <i>zona</i> or profaned (<i>ḥalala</i>) they shall not take, and a woman divorced from her husband they shall not take, for he is holy to his God.\" The Sages learned that a convert woman too is included among the women forbidden to kohanim, since she was not preserved in the holiness of Israel (Yevamot 60b; 61a; SA EH 6:8).</p><p>But a daughter of converts whose conception and birth were in holiness is fit to marry a kohen. However, after the Temple was destroyed and the Sanhedrin, which dealt with clarifying the lineage of the kohanim, ceased, the kohanim adopted the practice of being stringent with themselves and refrained from marrying a woman who is a descendant of converts on both sides. But if a kohen married a daughter of converts, they are not instructed to divorce, since by the letter of the law it is permitted (Kiddushin 78b; SA EH 7:21).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The Sages said in the Mishna (Yevamot 61a) that the prohibition of a convert woman to a kohen is included in the prohibition of <i>zona</i> stated in the Torah (Vayikra 21:7): \"A woman who is a zona or profaned they shall not take.\" The Tanna'im disagreed in Mishna Kiddushin 77b as to which convert woman is included in the prohibition: according to Rabbi Yehuda, a daughter of a male convert and a Jewish woman is forbidden to a kohen, but a daughter of a Jewish man and a female convert is permitted to a kohen. According to Rabbi Eliezer ben Ya'akov, when the father and mother are converts, the daughter is forbidden to a kohen, but if one of them is a born Jew, she is permitted. And according to Rabbi Yosei, even if both her parents are converts she is permitted to a kohen, provided that her conception was after the conversion. In the Gemara there, 78a, the view of Rashbi (Rabbi Shimon bar Yoḥai) is cited, that if they converted her before she reached the age of three, she is permitted to a kohen. All the views learned their words from the verse (Yeḥezkel 44:22): \"And a widow or a divorcée they shall not take as wives, but only virgins of the seed of the house of Israel.\" And the halakha follows Rabbi Yosei (there 78b), that any woman whose conception was in non-Jewishness is unfit for a kohen, and one whose mother conceived her as a Jew is permitted to a kohen.<br>If it is known that the convert woman had relations while she was a non-Jew, her prohibition is from the Torah. The question is whether this is the law also when she is a virgin. From the words of many Rishonim it is implied that even a virgin is forbidden to a kohen by Torah law. So it emerges from the words of Maharam of Rothenburg (Yevamot 61a); the Rosh (Yevamot 6:6); Tosafot (Yevamot 61a, s.v. \"ein\"), the Semag (negative commandments 121), the Mordekhai (Yevamot, remez 46), Rabbi Avraham Min Ha-har (Yevamot 61a), who explained that the prohibition is because non-Jews as a rule do not guard themselves from promiscuity, and therefore their children are considered \"children of promiscuity.\" And the same law applies if she converted before the age of three. And so it is implied from the Ritva (Yevamot 60b), who explained that the verse in Yeḥezkel interprets the Torah prohibition, that any woman who was not conceived in Israel is not included among \"virgins of the seed of the house of Israel,\" and is forbidden to a kohen. Apparently the reason for this is that non-Jews do not reject promiscuity and do not guard themselves from it as Israel does. According to the Ra'avad (16b) and the Rashba (Yevamot 60b) too, the prohibition of a virgin convert is from the Torah, except that in their view she is not included in the prohibition of zona; rather, the prohibition is a <i>halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai</i> (a law given to Moshe at Sinai), and Yeḥezkel attached it to the verse.<br>However, from the words of Rashi (Yevamot 61a, s.v. \"ela\") it emerges that only if she had relations with a non-Jew while she was a non-Jew is she forbidden to a kohen by Torah law, and it is implied that if she did not have relations, her prohibition is rabbinic; and so many wrote in explaining Rashi's view (Rashba and Ritva there; Rav Herzog in Heikhal Yitzḥak EH 1:18; Yabi'a Omer vol. 7 EH 11). And so it is implied from Tosafot Yeshanim (Yevamot 61a). And the same law applies in their view if she converted before the age of three, that the prohibition is rabbinic. But if she converted after the age of three, even when it is not known whether she had relations, she is forbidden because of a presumption (<i>ḥazaka</i>) that presumably she had relations, and it is implied that the prohibition is from the Torah. So wrote Rashi (Kiddushin 69a), the Rivan (Shitat Kadmonim, Yevamot 61b), the Ran (on the Rif, Kiddushin 30a), and the Nimukei Yosef (Yevamot 19a). On the other hand, some understood from Rashi that even when it is known that she did not have relations, or that she converted under the age of three, she is forbidden by Torah law; and so wrote Rav Goren (Terumat Ha-goren 1:24) and Rav Elyashiv (Notes on Kiddushin 69a).<br>Some also learned from the words of the Rambam (Hilkhot Issurei Bi'ah 16:1-2) that if she did not have relations, the prohibition is rabbinic (Arukh La-ner, Yevamot 76a; Yabi'a Omer vol. 7 EH 11); but on the other hand, many wrote that according to the Rambam too the prohibition is from the Torah in every case (Kiryat Sefer, Mirkevet Ha-mishneh, Beit Shmuel 6:20, Minḥat Ḥinukh 266:16; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 6:22; and so it is implied from the Maggid Mishneh and the Tosafot Yom Tov).<br>As stated, it is explained in Kiddushin 78b that the halakha follows Rabbi Yosei, that any woman whose conception was in non-Jewishness is unfit for a kohen. But if her conception was after her parents converted, she is fit for a kohen; however, they added: \"From the day the Temple was destroyed, the kohanim conducted themselves with distinction (<i>silsul</i>),\" recommending conducting oneself according to the view of Rabbi Eliezer ben Ya'akov, not to marry a daughter both of whose parents are converts, even after ten generations, as long as she is a descendant of converts on all sides. But if he has already married a daughter of converts, they do not divorce. And so it was ruled in SA EH 7:21 (the reason for the distinction is because of the cessation of the Sanhedrin – Yad Ramah and Ritva). Rav Yehoshua Ehrenberg, in Responsa Devar Yehoshua vol. 3 EH 8, wrote that if a daughter of a non-Jewish man and a Jewish woman became bound to a kohen \"with cords of love, and became engaged to him, and is already about to be married,\" one should be lenient because of the great need, which is considered like <i>be-di'avad</i>. Thus far. According to this, there is room to be lenient in any situation in which it is very difficult for them to separate."}]},{"slug":"19-08-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-10/","title_he":"י – שם האב בכתובה","title_en":"The Father's Name in the Ketuba","body_en":"<p>The custom is to write in the ketuba of converts: \"So-and-so son of Avraham Avinu\" or \"So-and-so daughter of Avraham Avinu.\" But if the groom or the bride converted with their parents at a young age, and they are embarrassed and want the name of their father, who begot them and converted with them and raised them, to be written in the ketuba, one may <i>le-khatḥila</i> write his name.</p><p>Similarly, parents who adopted a child and converted him, and the child or his adoptive father are embarrassed at having it written in the ketuba that he is the son of \"Avraham Avinu,\" one may <i>le-khatḥila</i> write in the ketuba the name of his adoptive father.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The custom of writing the convert's name in a get as \"son of Avraham Avinu\" began in the period of the Rishonim. Apparently, before that they did not write his father's name, as emerges from the Rashba (Responsa Rashba 2:16). Therefore the Ra'avya needed to explain in a responsum (939) why they wrote in a certain get the convert's name as \"son of Avraham\": because Avraham is the father of the nations. Some ruled to write \"son of Avraham\" (Responsa of the Rosh 15:4), and some ruled to write \"son of Avraham Avinu\" (Responsa Maharil 102), and the Mahari Mintz explained that if they write only \"Avraham\" without its being understood that the reference is to a convert, it will appear to be a falsehood (Seder Ha-get 103). In practice, SA EH 129:20 wrote that one writes in a get \"So-and-so son of Avraham Avinu.\" And some say that if he wrote only \"So-and-so son of Avraham,\" it is invalid (Levush 20, Beit Shmuel 39). And in Responsa Har Tzvi (EH 148:13) he wrote that the practice is to validate it <i>be-di'avad</i>.<br>A ketuba is in principle written like a get, as the Naḥalat Shiv'a wrote (Shtarot 12:16), so that people will not learn from it a mistake for a get. And so many wrote in practice, to write in a convert's ketuba \"son of Avraham Avinu\" (Minḥat Yitzḥak 1:136; Mishneh Halakhot 4:167; Naḥalat Tzvi by Rav Felder vol. 1, p. 112; Tzur Ya'akov 1:33). However, Shevet Ha-Levi (4:174) wrote that if they want to write the name of the adoptive father, it is permitted, since even in a get it is possible that this does not invalidate it. And so ruled Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach (Halikhot Shlomo 12:18, note 26), that if the groom is embarrassed, one may write the name of his adoptive father. And Rav Uziel wrote that even with regard to a get one may call him by the name of his convert father (Mishpetei Uziel vol. 2 YD 59).<br>And one may ask: why are people hurt by this today, whereas in the past they were not hurt? Possibly this stems from the fact that in the past those gathered at weddings were few, and they were mainly members of the close circle who knew that he was a convert, and therefore there was no shame in it. And perhaps in the past people were accustomed to express themselves more directly and bluntly, and consequently the convert was less offended at being called \"son of Avraham Avinu.\" In any case, although the stature of converts is very great, today many of the children are pained by this, and likewise the parents who toiled in raising their children may be very hurt; therefore it is fitting <i>le-khatḥila</i> to write the name of the father who converted or adopted."}]},{"slug":"19-08-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/19-08-11/","title_he":"יא – שם אביו לעלייה לתורה","title_en":"His Father's Name for Being Called to the Torah","body_en":"<p>According to the custom of those of Ashkenazic origin and some of those of Sephardic origin, when a person is called up to the Torah, he is called by his name and his father's name, for example: \"Reuven son of Ya'akov,\" and the custom is that if he is a convert, he is called \"Reuven son of Avraham,\" after Avraham Avinu, who is the father of all converts (Rema OḤ 139:3). Similarly, when the gabbai or the rabbi says a \"Mi She-berakh\" for one called up to the Torah, the custom is that he mentions his name and his father's name, and if he is a convert, the custom is to say \"son of Avraham.\"</p><p>However, when this causes distress to the convert or to his father, he should be called by the name of the father who raised him. For example, if the one called up to the Torah converted with his parents, and he does not want anyone to understand from this that he is a convert. Or his father may be pained at his name not being mentioned, for after he begot him and raised him, and brought him to convert with him, he wants him to be mentioned as his father. Likewise, when parents adopted a non-Jewish infant, the adoptive father may be pained that after all the effort he invested in raising and educating his son, the son is not called by his name. In all these cases, one should mention the name of the father who begot him and converted with him, or who adopted him.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"In Darkhei Moshe OḤ 139:1, and the Rema there, 3, he wrote, as something self-evident, that the custom is to call the convert to the Torah by the name \"So-and-so son of Avraham.\" And the Pri Megadim (Mishbetzot Zahav 1) wrote that perhaps here too one should add \"Avraham Avinu.\" However, this is not the practice, so as not to hurt the convert, lest he be offended by the mention of his being a convert. And although Rav Uziel (Mishpetei Uziel vol. 2 YD 59) holds that there is praise in his being a convert, in practice many do not want it mentioned that they are converts, especially one who converted at a young age, and therefore he is called \"So-and-so son of Avraham.\"<br>If the convert is adopted, or converted with his father and wants to be called by his father's name, some hold that even in this case he should be called \"So-and-so son of Avraham,\" for if he is called by his father's name, people will mistakenly write so in a get as well, and will forget that he is a convert (Minḥat Yitzḥak 1:136; Mishneh Halakhot 4:167; and so wrote Tzur Ya'akov 1:33 regarding an adopted son).<br>However, we have already learned in the previous note that even regarding a ketuba one may call the son by the name of his convert or adoptive father. And so wrote, regarding being called up to the Torah, Responsa Lev Aryeh 1:55 and Igrot Moshe YD 1:161, and so ruled Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach (Halikhot Shlomo 12:18). And so wrote Rav Uziel (Mishpetei Uziel vol. 2 YD 59) and Rav Felder (Naḥalat Tzvi vol. 1, p. 124), regarding a case where his father also converted, that it is permitted to call the son by his father's name."}]}]});
